From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Playport <dev@playport.invalid>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:10:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] audio: give an i386 child the iOS audio driver through a
 WoW64 table

0072 failed MemoryWineLoadUnixLibByNameWow64 for the audio drivers,
since the driver's table read an i386 mmdevapi's 32-bit parameter blocks
as 64-bit ones, so Portal 2 had no audio device. audio_wow64_ios.h now
gives the driver a WoW64 table in mmdevapi's unix_funcs order: it
mirrors the PE32 parameter blocks (stream handles stay 8-byte tokens,
event handles stay handles), converts their inner pointers through the
owner's window with checked ranges, and passes a stream call on only
for a stream its own window created. The by-name WoW64 query answers
with that table; the native table and its callers are unchanged.

A WoW64 stream's render scratch, which GetBuffer hands to the guest, is
a zeroed read-write view in the owner's window below 2 GiB
(ios_wow64_audio_alloc), so the guest writes the bytes that
ReleaseBuffer copies into the ring; RemoteIO still reads only the ring.
Release and growth free it through the owner and window recorded at
allocation. Process detach unpublishes the window's streams and frees
their scratch; the window's final teardown in 0073 unpublishes any left,
with no VM call under the windows lock (the view deletion reclaims the
scratch). The stream registry's mutex is initialized statically so a
second process attach cannot reinitialize it while it is in use.
Capture, loopback, properties and MIDI keep their no-device answers.

Class: ios-port
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-04-portal2-gameplay.md
Offered-upstream: no; the RemoteIO driver and the window exist only on the iOS port
